--TEST--
An `inout` argument and an element write through a union of classes are refused as they are through the one class that gives the property hooks — `rule:types/a-member-every-class-of-a-union-declares`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Box {
    public array<string> $tags = [];
    public string $name = "box";
    public function constructor() { }
}
final class Circle {
    public array<string> $tags { get => $this->tags; set { $this->tags = $value; } }
    public string $name { get => $this->name; set { $this->name = $value; } }
    public function constructor() { $this->tags = []; $this->name = "circle"; }
}
final class Text {
    public static function grow(inout string $text): void { $text = $text . "+"; }
}

Box|Circle $shape = new Circle();

// `Circle::$name` has hooks, so it cannot be passed to an `inout` parameter.
Text::grow(inout $shape->name);

// `Circle::$tags` has hooks, so an element cannot be written through it.
$shape->tags[] = "round";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0439]: a property with hooks cannot be passed to an `inout` parameter
   --> %s:19:18
   |
19 | Text::grow(inout $shape->name);
   |                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^ passed by reference here
   = help: reading it runs its `get` hook, and writing it runs its `set` hook. Read it into a variable, pass the variable, and assign the result back

error[E0478]: an array element cannot be written through the hooked property `tags`
   --> %s:22:1
   |
22 | $shape->tags[] = "round";
   | ^^^^^^^^^^^^ reading this runs its `get` hook
   = help: a property with hooks is read and written through its hooks. Read it into a variable, write the element there, and assign the variable back to the property

error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
